  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMidday radar imagery depicted widespread light rain across\neastern zones, ahead of one last trailing shortwave. Otherwise\nstratus was widespread across all of SW KS, and stratus is\nexpected to hold through this afternoon, tonight and into Sunday\nmorning. Once the final exiting shortwave departs this\nafternoon, only some patchy drizzle will remain through tonight.\nNBM temperatures this afternoon were several degrees too warm in\nthe stratus and drizzle/wet soils; many location will remain in\nthe 50s. \n\nStratus will persist through tonight. Stratus and areas of fog\nand/or drizzle will work against radiational cooling, with\ntemperatures slowly falling through the 50s most of tonight at\nmost locations. The mild fall continues; the normal sunrise\ntemperatures for the first morning of November is mid to upper\n30s. \n\nTwo strong shortwaves from the polar and subtropical jet streams\nare consistently forecast by the models to merge into one strong\nclosed midlevel cyclone over the Great Basin by 6 pm Sunday.\nRidge axis passing overhead 6 am Sunday will move east quickly,\nwith SWly midlevel flow establishing quickly Sunday afternoon.\nSouth winds will respond rapidly, averaging 20-30 mph with gusts\nnear 40 mph Sunday afternoon. Despite the improved mixing, and\n2-3C of warming at 850 mb, strong moisture advection will keep\nthe boundary layer saturated, or nearly so, through Sunday\nafternoon, with stratus prevailing. As such, coordinated with\nneighbors to reduce NBM temperatures 2-3 degrees for Sunday. By\nthe same token, Sunday night and Monday morning will be\nunseasonably mild beneath the strong low level jet, struggling\nto below 60 degrees (normal is mid 30s). \n\nIncoming strong trough is modelled to be quite progressive,\nreaching the Four Corners 6 am Monday, and near Elkhart 6 pm\nMonday. Elevated showers and thunderstorms are expected to\naccompany a strong low level jet Sunday night/early Monday, \nand these NBM pops up to the likely category were accepted. \nThe cyclone's dry slot is expected to keep most of SW KS dry \nfor most of Monday, with elevated strong to southwest winds. \nGiven some insolation, afternoon temperatures in the lower to \nmid 70s look reasonable. \n\nSurface and midlevel cyclones will reach the Medicine Lodge\nvicinity by 6 am Tuesday, allowing the strong cold front to\nrace through SW KS Monday night with intense north winds.\nNocturnal timing will reduce momentum transfer some, but\nNBM winds Monday night are too weak. NW winds in the grids \nfor Garden City at midnight are about 1/2 of the projected\nvalues from the 12z MAV (30 kts sustained, versus 15 kts NBM). \nThe strongest north winds from this system are expected\nimmediately after mixing is achieved midmorning to midday\nTuesday, when 850 mb winds near 2k ft AGL are 50-60 mph. \nEarly Tuesday is most likely when high wind warning criteria\nwill be achieved, and this wind will be accompanied by\nwidespread wind-blown rain in the cyclone's deformation. \nNBM pops do look plausible here with the rain early Tuesday, \nin the likely to definite category (70-90%). Given the\nprogressive nature of the parent trough, and the short \nresidency time, rain amounts Monday night through early Tuesday\nshould be restricted to less than 1/2 inch for most of  \nSW KS. Strong northwest winds persist Tuesday, with temperatures\nreduced to the 50s. \n\nWeak high pressure ridging arrives Tuesday night, allowing for\nmuch more seasonable temperatures in the mid 30s to near 40\nWednesday morning, but this incoming cool air mass is of Pacific\norigin, and will not be cold enough for a widespread frost or\nfreeze. \n\nDry quiet pleasant fall weather is expected Wednesday through\nFriday, along with temperatures not too far away from early\nNovember normals. The next shortwave trough will likely bring\nthe next round of rain next Saturday.",
